TLS material directory.

The server always reads its key store and trust store from this directory:

  conf/security/keystore.p12    server certificate and private key
  conf/security/truststore.p12  client certificate authorities (mTLS)
  conf/security/crypto.key      key ring for arbiter crypto (generated on first start)
  conf/security/users.dat       encrypted users and password hashes
                                (created with an initial admin on first start)
  conf/security/initial-admin.txt  one-time initial admin credentials (0600)

The server generates crypto.key on its first start when it is missing. It is
the only persistent server key: token signing and user-store encryption use
separate subkeys derived with HKDF-SHA256. crypto.key is a key ring:

  version=1
  active=k1
  key.k1=<Base64 32 bytes>

Rotate by adding key.k2 and setting active=k2; keep old keys so existing
ciphertext remains decryptable. The TLS paths are not configurable. Passwords are set in conf/server.properties:

  arbiter.server.tls.keystore.password
  arbiter.server.tls.truststore.password

Enable TLS with arbiter.server.tls.enabled=true and client certificates with
arbiter.server.tls.client.auth=true (mTLS). Keep conf/server.properties
readable only by the service account, and never commit real key material to a
source checkout.

When users.dat is missing, the first start creates an admin user with a random
256-bit password. The credentials are written once to
`conf/security/initial-admin.txt` with owner-only permissions; delete that file
after reading it. The user document is encrypted with AES-256-GCM using a
subkey derived from crypto.key. users.dat contains only a self-describing ciphertext envelope.
Later starts reuse crypto.key and users.dat. Back them up together. Delete
users.dat to create a new initial administrator on the next start. Existing
plaintext users.json and the previous temporary users.key format migrate
automatically during startup.
